From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: do not let a kernel echo clear a neighbour's local-inactive state

With EVPN-MH, a neighbour becomes locally inactive when its MAC leaves the
local dataplane, and that is what turns the MAC-IP route into a proxy route
so the ES peers can start the hold timer that retires the MAC.

That state cannot survive today. Advertisement of non-REACHABLE neighbours
is deliberately left enabled for SONiC, because traffic is forwarded by the
ASIC rather than the kernel and kernel neighbour entries go STALE while the
host is perfectly alive. The side effect is in netlink_neigh_change():

	if (zebra_evpn_mh_do_adv_reachable_neigh_only())
		local_inactive = !(ndm->ndm_state & NUD_LOCAL_ACTIVE);
	else
		local_inactive = false;

so every kernel neighbour update now reports local_inactive as false
unconditionally. zebra_evpn_local_neigh_update() takes that at face value
and clears ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE, which undoes the state zebra itself
set moments earlier.

Observed on hardware as a loop. After the MAC aged out, zebra marked the
neighbour inactive, advertised the MAC-IP with the proxy bit and installed
the entry inactive. BGP echoed the MAC-IP straight back as a sync path with
no proxy bit, the kernel update cleared the flag, and the neighbour was
re-advertised as active:

  sync-neigh ... local-inactive on mac del f 0x281
  Send MACIP Add f Proxy state 1 ... to bgp
  dp-install sync-neigh ... f 0x281 static inactive
  Recv sync-MACIP ADD ... flags 0x40 ... from bgp
  local neigh ... f 0x81 ... flag-update
  Send MACIP Add f None state 1 ... to bgp

Both leaves did this to each other, so neither ever converged and the MAC
was never withdrawn.

Only clear the flag when there is real evidence of local activity: either
the NUD state is a usable signal, or the neighbour's MAC is itself still
locally active. The MAC is driven by ASIC learning and ageing, so it is the
trustworthy source here. This keeps the neighbour inactive while its MAC is
gone and clears it again as soon as the MAC is relearnt.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
index 782201258c..ddb933e8f2 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
@@ -1598,10 +1598,18 @@ int zebra_evpn_local_neigh_update(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 							      : zmac->loc_seq;
 	}
 
-	if (local_inactive)
+	if (local_inactive) {
 		SET_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
-	else
+	} else if (zebra_evpn_mh_do_adv_reachable_neigh_only() ||
+		   !CHECK_FLAG(zmac->flags, ZEBRA_MAC_LOCAL_INACTIVE)) {
+		/* With reachable-only advertisement disabled, local_inactive is
+		 * hardcoded false for every kernel update and carries no
+		 * liveness information, so it must not clear a neighbour whose
+		 * MAC has already aged out of the local dataplane. The MAC is
+		 * the trustworthy signal here; the NUD state is not.
+		 */
 		UNSET_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
+	}
 
 	/* Mark Router flag (R-bit) */
 	if (is_router)
